        "pageDescription": "Enter the 2026 ArcGIS StoryMaps Competition. Get competition details and find inspiration for your story in our online gallery.",
        "evidenceText": "2026 ArcGIS StoryMaps Competition 2026 ArcGIS StoryMaps Competition Maps are for the bold Submit your story by December 11, 2026. Learn more about the competition Global storytelling competition Esri is pleased to announce the 2026 ArcGIS StoryMaps Competition. Take a geographic approach The world’s greatest challenges—including climate change, sustainability, and social and economic inequality—are interrelated and inherently tied to geography. GIS tools help people understand those challenges and communicate solutions through place-based stories. Learn about GIS Tell stories with maps Maps are an integral part of storytelling—a story can effect change, influence opinion, and create awareness. ArcGIS StoryMaps enables storytellers to easily combine text, maps, and interactive content to make engaging stories that inform and inspire. Browse storytelling resources Tell your geospatial story with ArcGIS Living Atlas content ArcGIS Living Atlas is the foremost collection of geographic information from around the globe. It includes layers, maps, apps, and tools to support your work. Explore featured apps or search all ArcGIS Living Atlas content for your story. Get started with ArcGIS Living Atlas Previous Next Show More Maps are for the bold Bold makes the world better. And, as Esri President Jack Dangermond said, “Better is what we all want. For ourselves, for our organizations, for our communities.” Bold is the urban planner who refuses to believe “that’s just the way cities are.” It’s the disaster response team that refuses to leave anyone behind. It’s the leader who acts with clarity—and insight—instead of impulse. How are you bold in pursuit of better? Esri invites storytellers age 18 and up to answer that question. Submit a story created with ArcGIS StoryMaps that addresses one of the following categories. Seeing What Others Missed : Where mapping revealed an unexpected pattern or insight Refusing the Status Quo : Where maps helped challenge “the way it’s always been done” Designing a Better Future : Using geography to anticipate, test, or prepare for what’s next Acting with Clarity in Complexity : Where maps helped leaders make deliberate, informed decisions amid uncertainty Explore the announcement story Connect place and story Create a narrative that links people, places, and data in a way others can understand. Key dates Recognition Requirements Judging criteria Terms and conditions Key dates and submission deadlines SUBMISSION DEADLINE December 11, 2026, 5:00 p.m. (PT) FINALISTS ANNOUNCED February 9, 2027 WINNERS ANNOUNCED April of 2027 Follow @ArcGISStoryMaps on X for ongoing competition resources and announcements. Recognition All winners will: Receive an Esri certificate Be featured on the Esri website and in an interview Receive an award at Esri’s 2027 User Conference Each winner may accept a voucher to take an Esri Technical Certification Exam : one (1) per individual or two (2) per team. Each winner will receive a signed printed copy of The Power of Where : one (1) per individual or two (2) per team. Requirements Applicants 18 years and older globally may enter the competition once in one of four categories: Seeing What Others Missed Refusing the Status Quo Designing a Better Future Acting with Clarity in Complexity Each category will include a student track. An applicant may be an individual or a group submitting one entry. Entries must be built with ArcGIS StoryMaps. Judging criteria Esri will select up to 8 finalists for each category. From the finalists, the guest judges will select one winner and one student winner in each category. Esri also will recognize one winner of each special award below: Community Choice Award Storytelling with Maps Innovation Award Terms and conditions By entering the ArcGIS StoryMaps competition, you must agree to the terms and conditions. Review terms and conditions Meet the guest judges Get to know your 2026 ArcGIS StoryMaps Competition judges. Latinos in Heritage Conservation Karina Amalbert Karina is the geospatial project manager at Latinos in Heritage Conservation, where she leads the Abuelas Project’s GIS online platform. Geospatial Equity Tech and Afritech Véronique Couttee Véronique—founder of Geospatial Equity Tech and winner of the National Geographic Wayfinder Award—is a leadership, impact, and equity specialist. UT Chattanooga Charlie Mix Charlie serves as the GIS director at University of Tennessee at Chattanooga and founder of UTC’s Center for Applied Geospatial Data Science. Esri Lauren Tierney Lauren—former senior graphics reporter and Cartographer at The Washington Post—is an Esri senior product manager for mapping and cartography. Chief Scientist of Esri Dr. Dawn Wright Dawn aids in strengthening the scientific foundation for Esri software and services and represents Esri to the scientific community. Previous Next Show More Resources Find tips and tricks to get you started. Learn GIS fundamentals Start with Introduction to geospatial storytelling Get to know ArcGIS StoryMaps with tutorials Use narrative structures for stronger storytelling Make great stories with StoryScape magazine Browse the ArcGIS StoryMaps gallery Explore ArcGIS StoryMaps learning resources Previous Next Show More Get inspiration for your story Browse the 2025 winners Visit the gallery Build your story with ArcGIS StoryMaps Sign in with an ArcGIS account Don't have an Esri account? Create one now opens in new window",
