        "pageTitle": "Information design principles - Data Visualization - LibGuides at York University",
        "pageDescription": "LibGuides: Data Visualization: Information design principles",
        "evidenceText": "Information design principles - Data Visualization - LibGuides at York University Skip to Main Content Search this Guide Search Data Visualization: Information design principles Home Reading data visualizations Types of data visualizations Examples How visualizations lie Citing data visualizations Creating a data visualization Creation process Choosing the right visualization tool Information design principles Accessibility Equity, diversity, and inclusion Finding data Resources Gestalt principles Gestalt Principles of Design The Gestalt Principles were first created in the 1920s to describe how human perception naturally tries to find order, grouping elements and finding patterns. By understanding these principles, design and thus data visualizations can be created to be both aesthetically pleasing and easily understood. The Gestalt Principles consist of: Proximity: White space Similarity: Objects that look similar are instinctively grouped together Enclosure: Helps distinguish between groups Symmetry: Objects should not be out of balance, or missing, or wrong Closure: We tend to complete shapes and paths even if part of them is missing Continuity: We tend to continue shapes beyond their ending points Connection: Helps group elements together Figure and ground: We see foreground first    Resources : Gestalt Principles from the Interaction Design Foundation   Overview of gestalt principles with images to visualize each principle Gestalt Principles: The Complete Overview   By the UX Cam blog Marks and channels Data visualizations are built using marks and channels and understanding how to use them properly for visual encoding information is critical. Marks:  basic geometric elements that depict items or links in a data visualization Source: Tamara Munzner, 2014   Channels:  visual variables that control the appearance of marks in a data visualization Source: Tamara Munzner, 2014   Resources Munzner. (2015). Marks and Channels. In  Visualization Analysis and Design  (pp. 95&ndash;116). A K Peters/CRC Press. https://doi.org/10.1201/b17511-5. View online . Colour usage Proper usage of colour helps viewers quickly process a data visualization, understand the meaning, and remember its impact. Colour choice is extremely important in making data visualizations accessible . Some tools like Tableau and Excel have default colour schemes but this does not always mean that they are optimal.   Colours should be used to highlight the purpose of the data visualization: If data is categorical, then visually distinct colours should be used.  If data is numerical and continuous, then a sequential colour palette, a graduation of colours, should be used (ex. a pale green to a deep forest green) If data is numerical and consists of two extremes of positive and negative values with a baseline in the middle (ex. temperatures), then a diverging colour scheme could be used (ex. blue to red, for cold to hot temperatures)   Resources on colour theory and best practices: Which color scale to use when visualizing data , a 4 part series by the Datawrapper blog How to pick more beautiful colors for your data visualizations  by the Datawrapper blog, which uses hue, saturation, and brightness/value to explain which colours and which colour combinations elevate your data visualizations Picking the right colors by Mike Cisneros on Storytelling with Data, which suggests the best colour palette to highlight a comparison between two, three, four, or more categories Color for data visualization , part of Adobe's principles to create better designs   Colour palette picking tools: Color Brewer 2 , provides a selection of palettes based on inputted number of data class and type of palette desired Viz Palette , creates a report diagnosing the difficulties in telling inputted colours apart Data color picker , can provide a customized palette for categorical data, a single hue sequential palette, or a divergent palette based on a user-chosen colour Last Updated: Aug 6, 2026 8:47 AM URL: https://researchguides.library.yorku.ca/datavisualization Print Page Login to LibApps Subjects: Finding Types of Information Multidisciplinary",
