        "evidenceText": "11th Future Technologies Conference 2026 - Berlin | Converging AI, Quantum, Robotics, Cybersecurity, and Beyond Skip to main content 15-16 October 2026 · NYX Hotel Berlin Köpenick, Berlin, Germany 11 th Future Technologies Conference 2026 15-16 October 2026 in Berlin, Germany Call for Papers Register Now Watch video 11th Edition 60+ Countries represented 25 Published proceedings 10 Past editions online About the Future Technologies Conference Converging AI, Quantum, Robotics, Cybersecurity, and Beyond The Future Technologies Conference (FTC) has run annually since 2016, now marking its 11th edition, bringing together researchers, academics, and industry innovators to examine the technologies shaping the coming decade - from artificial intelligence and quantum computing to robotics, cybersecurity, and immersive systems. The conference provides a rigorous platform for peer-reviewed research, interdisciplinary exchange, and translating emerging ideas into practical impact through keynotes, technical sessions, and interactive discussions across academic and industrial domains. A decade in, FTC continues to shape the discourse on future technologies grounded in evidence, relevance, and societal need Call for Papers Register Now Host city Berlin NYX Hotel Berlin Köpenick Venue and travel Keynote talks Invited talks from leading researchers and practitioners in the field. Past speakers across SAI conferences have included researchers from Imperial College London, RWTH Aachen University, University of Amsterdam, University of Oxford, MIT, Stanford, Google, and Microsoft Research. Paper sessions Oral and poster presentations of peer-reviewed work. Virtual participation is available for authors unable to travel. Awards Outstanding papers and posters are recognised at the closing of the conference. Several award categories acknowledge distinguished contributions across research quality, innovation, and scholarly presentation. Networking Meet researchers and industry experts from 60+ countries across academia, research institutes, and industry. Selective peer review 37&ndash;39% acceptance rate across all conferences, reflecting double-blind review by an international programme committee. Co-located Events Held alongside 11th Future Technologies Conference 2026, sharing its dates, venue and proceedings. Future of Human-Computer Interaction Conference (FHCI) Learn more Future of Information and Computing Conference (FICC) Learn more Future of Intelligent and Autonomous Agents Conference (FIAA) Learn more Conference Tracks Choose the track that fits your research when you submit. Topic lists are indicative, and work across the field is welcome. Machine Learning 10 topics Deep Learning Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems Generative AI Explainable AI Large Language Models (LLMs) Computer Vision Reinforcement Learning Applied AI AI in Healthcare AI for Climate and Sustainability View track details Human-Computer Interaction 10 topics VR, AR, MR & XR Multimodal Interfaces Wearable Computing Brain Computer Interfaces Human-AI Co-Creativity Inclusive Design Context-Aware Computing Spatial Computing Voice & Conversational Interfaces Haptic & Physiological Interfaces View track details Data Science 10 topics Data Mining Predictive Modeling Data Analytics Data Fusion Sentiment Analysis Natural Language Processing Information Retrieval Big Data Speech Technologies AI Augmented Data Science View track details Cyber-Physical Systems 10 topics Smart Cities Smart Grids and Energy Networks Sensing and Sensor Networks Ambient Assisted Living Digital Twins Intelligent Transportation Smart Manufacturing Internet of Things (IoT) Autonomous Systems Geographic Information Systems View track details Future Computing Architectures 10 topics Quantum Computing Neuromorphic Systems High Performance Computing Human-Centered Computing Cloud Computing Edge and Fog Computing GPU Programming Distributed Computing Cognitive Systems Complex and Adaptive Systems View track details Robotics 10 topics Human Robot Interaction Cognitive Robotics Robotic Prosthetics Swarm Intelligence Real-Time Learning Self-Constructing Systems Bio-Inspired Robotics Continual Learning in Robotics Neuro-Robotic Interfaces Industry 5.0 View track details Cybersecurity 10 topics Cybersecurity Frameworks Threat Intelligence Cryptography Quantum-Resistant Security Internet and Cloud Security Digital Identity and Biometrics Trusted Architectures Privacy-Preserving AI Blockchain Ethical AI View track details Education Technology 10 topics AI in Education e-Learning Tools Gamified Learning Virtual Learning Ecosystems Social Learning Technologies Curriculum Design Learning Analytics Personalized Learning Accessibility and Inclusivity Delivery Systems and Environments View track details Your research, published and indexed Accepted papers appear in peer-reviewed conference proceedings. Publication partner The proceedings will be published by Springer in the \" Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems \" series. These proceedings will also be submitted for indexing in major databases, including Web of Science, SCOPUS, INSPEC, WTI Frankfurt eG, zbMATH, and SCImago. (*) Papers from the late-breaking round will be published as a separate proceedings volume after the conference. Submitted for indexing in WTI Frankfurt eG Springer LNNS series (opens in a new tab) Past proceedings Call for papers Proceedings Papers from past editions are published in proceedings by Springer. Choose a year to open its volumes on the publisher's website. 2025 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Volume 3 (opens in a new tab) Volume 4 (opens in a new tab) 2024 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Volume 3 (opens in a new tab) Volume 4 (opens in a new tab) 2023 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Volume 3 (opens in a new tab) Volume 4 (opens in a new tab) 2022 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Volume 3 (opens in a new tab) Earlier proceedings (4 years) 2021 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Volume 3 (opens in a new tab) 2020 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Volume 3 (opens in a new tab) 2019 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) 2018 Springer Volume 1 (opens in a new tab) Volume 2 (opens in a new tab) Previous Editions Explore past editions of Future Technologies Conference. 2025 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 2019 2018 2017 2016 Past attendees include researchers from leading universities MIT Stanford University University of Oxford University of Cambridge Imperial College London RWTH Aachen University University of Amsterdam National University of Singapore University of Toronto ETH Zurich MIT Stanford University University of Oxford University of Cambridge Imperial College London RWTH Aachen University University of Amsterdam National University of Singapore University of Toronto ETH Zurich Past Speakers A selection of past keynote talks from Future Technologies Conference. 2025 Mario Trapp Fraunhofer IKS 2025 Stefan Wagner Technical University of Munich 2025 Gitta Kutyniok Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München 2025 Wojciech Samek TU Berlin 2024 Steven Pemberton CWI, Amsterdam 2024 Peter Triantafillou University of Warwick 2024 John Collomosse Adobe Research 2024 Adrian Hilton University of Surrey Meet the 2026 speakers More keynotes from 2025 Frequently Asked Questions How do I submit a paper? See the Call for Papers page for tracks, deadlines, formatting templates and submission instructions. What are the formatting guidelines? Prepare your paper with the conference Word or LaTeX template. Page limits, abstract length and figure and reference rules are in the formatting guidelines on the Call for Papers page. What is the review process? Every submission is double-blind peer reviewed by the Technical Programme Committee, judged on originality, technical depth, impact and clarity. Read the full review process and criteria . What's the policy on AI-assisted writing tools? Generative AI may support drafting and language editing, but its use must be declared in the manuscript, it cannot be listed as an author, and authors stay fully accountable for the content. See the full AI policy . Do you support visa letters for travel? Yes - registered, presenting authors can request an invitation/visa support letter. Contact us via the Contact page once registered. What's your registration and refund policy? Registration Categories Author: Authors are those presenting a paper at the conference. Each author registration allows presentation of one paper only, and includes access to keynotes, paper sessions, and all conference activities. Participant: Participants are attendees who do not submit or present a paper but wish to attend presentations, keynotes, and sessions. Payment Information Registration fees must be paid in advance. We accept most major international credit and debit cards. Payments can also be made via bank transfer. If you wish to pay by bank transfer, please contact us at [email protected] to request an invoice with the necessary payment details. Registration Policy Each registration is valid for one delegate only. Each author registration permits the presentation of one paper. All registrations are final and non-refundable. Registrations are non-transferable to another delegate. What People Say &ldquo;What an exciting and intellectually stimulating day at the Future Technologies Conference (FTC) 2026 in Berlin!\r\n\r\nA particular highlight for me was the fantastic keynote.\r\nI also enjoyed the discussions on smart healthcare and the challenges of robotics and automation, as well as the fascinating conversations over coffee and the thoughtful questions after my presentation. It is always rewarding to discover unexpected connections between different fields of research.\r\nA day full of ideas, curiosity and inspiring exchanges!&rdquo; Ilona Negro European Personnel Selection Office Read Full Review &ldquo;It was actually great and completely different from what I expected—I thought it might be a bit like white noise, where you’d fall asleep or end up on your phone, but it was genuinely interesting and engaging because the speakers came with very challenging subjects. They really challenged you to think about them. I felt there was so much there that I could take away. I know I was always asking questions at the end, but that’s precisely because it was actually interesting. The talks constantly stimulated my thinking and made me think, “Okay, I hadn’t seen it that way before.”&rdquo; Adrian Neal Capgemini &ldquo;Beyond presenting my research and hearing great keynotes, I was impressed by how intentionally the organizers created chances to connect: small tasks, smart questions, simple prompts that sparked real conversations, and feel at home in a room full of new people.\r\n\r\nIt turned the conference into something rare:\r\nengaging, energizing, and genuinely community-building.&rdquo; Leonie Reichardt BMW Motorrad Read Full Review &ldquo;For me, it was super cool that there was a short track on digital health, which, of course, I enjoyed very much. But it was also a great experience to take a step back and get a broader view of what’s going on in AI and in completely different industries. AI is such an amazing and rapidly growing field that there’s always a danger of getting stuck in your own area of digital health, like in my case. So, it was really good to take inspiration from other areas and industries, including security and all of that.&rdquo; Jan Kirchhoff DigiHealth Institute of Hochschule Neu-Ulm &ldquo;It was rewarding to see the talk spark engaged questions and thoughtful discussion. The keynotes and many of the paper sessions were excellent — with AI unsurprisingly front and centre. I came away with a much clearer sense of where work on neural networks and deep learning is heading. Equally valuable were the conversations in between sessions, connecting with people from both academia and industry.&rdquo; Jakob Lütkemeier Trinity College Dublin &ldquo;I can confidently say that these events have always been a great source of knowledge and inspiration. I would like to thank the organisers for consistently delivering such top‑notch conferences that bring together brilliant minds and foster meaningful discussions. I look forward to continuing the conversations sparked during the event and to seeing how the ideas we explored will grow in the months ahead.&rdquo; Reno Yuri Camilleri University of Malta &ldquo;A special thank you to SAI Conferences for an inspiring and highly engaging event, and to all colleagues who joined the session, asked thoughtful questions, and exchanged ideas on the future of digital transformation in higher education.&rdquo; Tiago H. Moreira de Oliveira NOVA IMS Photos from 2025 Social media aggregator → Explore Other SAI Conferences Part of the SAI Conferences portfolio, spanning computing, AI, and emerging technologies. Computer Vision Conference 15-16 April 2027 Mercure Amsterdam City Hotel Netherlands Learn more Computing Conference 8-9 July 2027 Clayton Hotel Chiswick United Kingdom Learn more Intelligent Systems Conference 2-3 September 2027 Mercure Amsterdam City Hotel Netherlands Learn more",
