        "evidenceText": "State of the Map US 2026 | OpenStreetMap US State of the Map US 2026 OpenStreetMap US hosted the 14th annual State of the Map US conference on June 11–13, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. Thank you to everyone who participated. Talks were recorded and will be available online soon. Sponsors Recorded Talks The Mappiest Event of the Year State of the Map US is the largest gathering of OpenStreetMap community members from across the country. It’s three days of mappers, businesses, academics, government agencies, and nonprofits all collaborating around the free and editable map of the world. Whether you’re brand new to open mapping or you’re an OpenStreetMap veteran, this conference is for you. Narrative Map Competition Results In partnership with Esri, OpenStreetMap US hosted our very first SOTMUS Narrative Map Competition! Congratulations to this year’s winners: Best Overall: “ Cyclist Survival Map ” by Atlas Guo Best example of Mapping for Your Community: “ Find Your Place ” by James Piacentini Best example of Mapping for Mobility: “ Rail Globe ” by Gareth Baldrica-Franklin Best example of Open Data for Resiliency: “ Divided by Highways ” by Paige Kiarie Best example of Open Data for Healthier Ecosystems: “ Scales in the Canals ” by Hareshram Sivaraman Talks Welcome to State of the Map US 2026 Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Maggie Cawley Kick off your State of the Map US with welcoming remarks from OSM US Executive Director Maggie Cawley. Welcome to Wisconsin Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Howard Veregin Wisconsin State Cartographer Howard Veregin welcomes State of the Map US to Madison. An Update from your Hosts, OpenStreetMap US Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Alyssa Castronuovo , Jake Low , and Maggie Cawley What’s cooking at OpenStreetMap US? Find out the latest happenings from the OSM US Staff - from program updates to tech and tools! From Sidewalk to Signal: Mapping Internet Access with Street-Level Imagery Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Felipe Valdez What if communities could map their own neighborhoods and control their digital futures? Philly Community Wireless and Temple Libraries combine Mapillary street-level imagery with OpenStreetMap to map infrastructure gaps and community assets.... Madison in 50 Maps: A Cartographic Tour through OSM Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Stephen Kennedy Take a visual tour of Madison through the lens of OpenStreetMap! Based on the #30DayMapChallenge and a forthcoming book, this session highlights several maps of our host city—discussing at least 3 or... Cartography on Autopilot: Turning GTFS and OSM into Beautiful Maps Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Kapeel Daryani MTC is creating an automated map production system that blends OSM with GTFS data and uses QGIS plugins to produce uniform, print ready transit maps. The workflow processes stops and routes from... TeenMaptivists: Open Mapping in High School Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Steven Johnson A thumbnail overview of the TeenMaptivist project: training the next generation of OpenStreetMappers. A Decade of Mapping Impact: Celebrating 10 Years of YouthMappers Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Chad Blevins In 2025, YouthMappers officially celebrates 10 years of empowering the next generation of open-source cartographers. What started as a vision to connect university students with meaningful geospatial projects has grown into a... State of the OSM cartography landscape Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Alan McConchie and Kelsey Taylor Creating a basemap using OSM data is easier than ever, but to design one from scratch, or to make substantial modifications to an existing basemap, requires a hodgepodge of different tools to... OSM US Tech Updates Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Jake Low OSM US maintains a number of useful tools in the OpenStreetMap ecosystem. Learn what’s new in MapRoulette, OSMCha, Layercake, the OSM US Tileservice, and more. Why Ground-Truthing is Important Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Rob Savoye This is a short talk about why ground-truthing is important. Incorrect or lacking tags often leads to navigation mistakes which would be better to not experience… In addition, in many small rural... PWG 1.0: Creating the Pedestrian Working Group Schema & Guide Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Jacob Hall and Amy Bordenave In this 15-minute presentation, OSM US Pedestrian Working Group participants Amy Bordenave and Jacob Hall will announce the 1.0 releases of the PWG Schema and PWG Guide, share our Working Group’s process... How Amazon Generates Map Tiles for Last-Mile Delivery Using OSM-Derived Datasets and Planetiler Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Vishal Kumar and Shaishav Maisuria Amazon Last Mile generates 22+ custom map tile layers using OSM-derived datasets like Overture Maps, Natural Earth, and lake centerlines. We re-architected our legacy PostGIS based pipeline, introducing Planetiler engine with GeoParquet... OSM-lish: OpenStreetMap's Own English Dialect Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Clay Smalley While OSM may have begun as a British project, the language we tag with is anything but. Come explore the evolution of OSM tagging since its 2004 inception, and the mishmash of... Review of American Roadways (ROAR) Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Matthew Whilden We have 10.5 million sections of roadway left to review from the original TIGER imports. Fortunately, we have more data sources available than ever before. What tools can we create to help... Dhanyasri's take on megacities climate resilience Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Dhanyasri Bolla As cities face increasing climate risks, researchers are racing to model resilience in megacities. While analyzing adaptation strategies across 43 megacities, I found that standard data often lacks the critical details on... Using OpenStreetMap data to assess the bikeability of schools Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Harald Kliems , Ben Varick , and Samuel Younkin Biking to school can have many health, environmental, and social benefits, but few students in the United States bike to school. In a community-engaged research partnership between UW-Madison, the Wisconsin Bike Fed,... Integrating OSM User Authentication into OldInsuranceMaps.net Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Adam Cox OldInsuranceMaps.net is a Django-based crowdsourcing platform with hundreds of users, but was built with basic account configuration–no social logins or external integrations. However, as interest in the project from the OSM and... MapLibre Tiles - next generation tiles and other MapLibre news Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Yuri Astrakhan Learn everything MapLibre has been up to, from the new tile format to Martin tile server, MapLibre GL, MapLibre Native, and many other projects Scoring the feasibility of micromobility with OSM data Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Julio May Springfield, IL, faces high car dependency and infrastructure gaps. This session explores using multi-criteria geospatial analysis to solve the “last-mile” problem. We’ll dive into a composite scoring model built on five key... Wikimedia Enterprise – Lessons learned from working with large commercial reusers Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Lane Becker and Michal Migurski Over the last 6 years, The Wikimedia Foundation has developed a separate infrastructure to handle large commercial reusers of Wikipedia (etc) content. And then AI came along… Having the legal, technical, and... The Walksheds Tool: Evaluating Pedestrian Accessibility Using OSM Data Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Sam Yasen The OpenSidewalks Schema is an open pedestrian transportation network data standard for describing and sharing pedestrian network and pedestrian network-adjacent data. See how The Walksheds tool (https://walksheds.tdei.us/) turns OpenStreetMap (OSM) sidewalks, crossings,... Community mapping, data sovereignty and Indigenous toponyms on OSM Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Emily Jacobi In 2015 Awana Digital (previously Digital Democracy) used OSM iD Editor to create Mapeo; an offline, open-source app co-built with Indigenous communities in the Amazon. Now with CoMapeo, people around the world... How We Prepared HIFLD for the Singularity Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Jeremy Herzog W