        "finalUrl": "https://www.niemanlab.org/2026/10/u-k-politicians-and-german-publishers-call-on-regulators-to-pause-metas-new-terms-before-october-30-deadline/",
        "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
        "pageTitle": "U.K. politicians and German publishers call on regulators to pause Meta’s new terms before October 30 deadline | Nieman Journalism Lab",
        "pageDescription": "The U.K. competition and consumer protection authority sent a formal letter to Meta to discuss its new commercial terms of service. On Tuesday, Will Hayter…",
        "evidenceText": "U.K. politicians and German publishers call on regulators to pause Meta’s new terms before October 30 deadline | Nieman Journalism Lab Get Nieman Lab in your inbox. All the latest headlines and insights from the world of digital journalism and publishing. Our daily newsletter is a must-read from newsrooms to executive suites. Leave this empty Email address Name (optional) Subscribe We will never sell your address. Prefer a weekly email? Nearly there &mdash; check your email. We have sent a confirmation link to . Click it and you are on the list. If it has not turned up in a minute or two, it may be in your spam folder. Subscribe another address Meta offices in Dublin, Ireland. (Noel/Adobe Stock License.) U.K. politicians and German publishers call on regulators to pause Meta’s new terms before October 30 deadline Link parliamentlive.tv ↗ Posted by Andrew Deck , Hanaa' Tameez October 21, 2026 The U.K. competition and consumer protection authority sent a formal letter to Meta to discuss its new commercial terms of service. On Tuesday, Will Hayter , the executive director for digital markets at the Competition and Markets Authority (CMA), shared the update during a session of the communications and digital committee of British Parliament , after a member of the House of Lords pressed him about Meta’s new terms. Hayter said he scheduled a meeting with the tech giant today to seek clarity on the new terms, though the CMA declined to provide any further details. RELATED ARTICLE Legal experts warn Meta’s new terms still grant broad scraping permissions, despite language changes Andrew Deck October 17, 2026 Last week, Nieman Lab broke the news that the terms would permit mass scraping of websites “associated with” business accounts on Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, and Threads. The new terms are slated to take effect on October 30, and there is currently no option to opt out. Virtually every news organization owns at least one Meta business account. “Are you going to pause the deadline? I mean, [it’s in] a week,” Veronica Wadley, Baroness Fleet and a former editor of the Evening Standard, said during the session of Parliament. “You know this includes almost every U.K. news publisher and it is clearly uncompetitive leverage of Meta’s strategic market position.” In the same session, Sarah Cardell , the CEO of CMA, said the authority had limited intervention options because it has not formally designated Meta as an “entrenched market power” (something it has done in the past for Google and Apple ). Cardell said other competition enforcement actions are a possibility, but the CMA is “urgently” seeking more information from Meta before making any judgment. Meta's controversial terms October 15 Meta’s new commercial terms will allow the scraping of news sites, legal experts say October 17 Legal experts warn Meta’s new terms still grant broad scraping permissions, despite language changes October 21 ★ U.K. politicians and German publishers call on regulators to pause Meta’s new terms before October 30 deadline October 22 Publishers pressure Meta to nix new terms that would let the company scrape their websites October 26 Meta delays rollout of terms that would allow broad scraping of news sites. Legal experts are still skeptical. October 27 “A gross abuse of Meta’s power”: California senator Adam Schiff tells Meta to scrap its new terms of service October 28 Meta pledges to consult publishers before finalizing its new terms Over the weekend, Meta removed the word “train” from its terms and limited the use of future scraped content to “advertising and business products.” But legal experts told Nieman Lab the revised terms would still permit mass scraping. A Meta spokesperson said on Saturday that the company revised the language because it “wasn’t clear in the initial consolidation of terms that robots.txt preferences are respected. News sites, like all other content owners, still have the ability to disable the crawler via robots.txt flags. That has been the case, and it has not changed.” Meta has not included any specific language about respecting robots.txt preferences in its revised terms of service. “Does Meta think we can’t read?” Theo Bamber , chief executive of the U.K.’s News Media Association, wrote in a LinkedIn post on Monday. “We know you’ve taken the word ‘train’ out but changing it to ‘develop’ isn’t fooling anyone.” News publisher associations across Europe have called on regulators to intervene before the terms take effect next week. On Tuesday, two major German publisher associations called on the European Commission to prohibit the new terms from going into effect until a complete review had been conducted. Together, the German Association of Free Press and the Federal Association of Digital Publishers represent more than 9,000 media brands across Europe. RELATED ARTICLE Meet the journalists training AI models for Meta and OpenAI Andrew Deck February 20, 2025 “Meta intends to automatically capture, copy, and store the complete websites and archives of publishers and analyze them for its own automated and non-automated commercial products,” reads a joint statement from the associations . “This makes access to Meta’s platforms contingent on the free surrender of the entire digital publishing product. This is blatant theft.” The statement alleges that Meta’s new terms could be in violation of the E.U. Digital Markets Act, which was passed in 2022 to curb the dominance of big tech companies and create fairer competition across the region. The publishers cited a specific clause that guarantees “fair, reasonable, and non-discriminatory general conditions of access for business users.” The uproar from European publishers echoes similar opposition to the new terms from major U.S. publisher associations. Jason Kint, the CEO of Digital Content Next, has been vocal about his skepticism of the new terms since Nieman Lab’s story broke last week. “Meta’s supposed Friday night correction to its new commercial terms is a farce,” he wrote in a statement published on Tuesday. “Exactly what you’d expect from a gatekeeper notorious for abusing its market power.” Get Nieman Lab&rsquo;s Daily Digest The most important stories about journalism and the future of news, delivered to your inbox every afternoon. Subscribe Andrew Deck is a staff writer covering AI at Nieman Lab. Have tips about how AI is being used in your newsroom? You can reach Andrew via email , Bluesky , or Signal (+1 203-841-6241). Hanaa' Tameez is a staff writer at Nieman Lab. You can reach her via email ( [email protected] ), Bluesky DM (@hanaatameez.bsky.social), or on Signal (@hanaatameez.01). October 21, 2026 See more on   AI & Journalism Tags agents AI anti trust Digital Content Next Digital Markets Act E.U. Facebook Germany Instagram meta publisher association regulation regulators scraping social media social media platforms terms of service Threads U.K. U.K. Parliament WhatsApp The latest from Nieman Lab Press Forward gave $20 million to 205 local newsrooms over two years. Here’s how some of them spent it. The unrestricted funding helped small outlets plug holes and try new things, but publishers ran up against the limits of what two $50,000 grants could accomplish, especially when it came to hiring. Sophie Culpepper Only 32% of Americans have high confidence that news orgs will report midterm election results accurately, survey finds Sarah Scire The Nieman Lab News-About-News Quiz: October 30, 2026 Need another reason to read that 47,212th article about paywall strategy? What if it earned you a point ? Joshua Benton The New York Times is adding more AI features to its Cooking app, and users aren’t thrilled Joshua Benton The Independent Journalism Atlas looks at “wells” and “springs” in news deserts Sophie Culpepper Foundation Reports Lab Storyboard Help advance the Nieman Foundation's mission “to promote and elevate the standards of journalism” by making a donation . 