        "evidenceText": "Meta delays rollout of terms that would allow broad scraping of news sites. Legal experts are still skeptical. | Nieman Journalism Lab Get Nieman Lab in your inbox. All the latest headlines and insights from the world of digital journalism and publishing. Our daily newsletter is a must-read from newsrooms to executive suites. Leave this empty Email address Name (optional) Subscribe We will never sell your address. Prefer a weekly email? Nearly there &mdash; check your email. We have sent a confirmation link to . Click it and you are on the list. If it has not turned up in a minute or two, it may be in your spam folder. Subscribe another address Meta delays rollout of terms that would allow broad scraping of news sites. Legal experts are still skeptical. Meta’s new commercial terms will go into effect three weeks later than initially planned. (Adobe Stock) By Hanaa' Tameez Oct. 26, 2026, 5 a.m. RELATED ARTICLE Legal experts warn Meta’s new terms still grant broad scraping permissions, despite language changes Andrew Deck October 17, 2026 Meta has delayed the effective date of its new terms of service for business accounts to November 20 in the face of mounting pressure from the global news industry. Nieman Lab first reported on the terms on October 15. They would allow for the mass scraping of websites “associated with” business accounts on Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, and Threads. Virtually every news organization has at least one business account. On October 16, Meta removed the word “train” from its terms and limited the use of future scraped content to “advertising and business products.” The terms were previously set to take effect on October 30, with no way to opt out, setting off an outcry from news publishers, politicians, and press associations on both sides of the Atlantic. “We are extending the effective date of our updated Commercial Terms and Self-Serve Ad Terms to November 20 to allow more time for us to explain to our partners and stakeholders what’s changing and what’s not — including how Meta uses web crawlers and the fact that content owners will still be able to use robots.txt to signal preferences,” a Meta spokesperson told Nieman Lab in a statement Friday. A Meta spokesperson said on October 17 that the company revised the language because it “wasn’t clear in the initial consolidation of terms that robots.txt preferences are respected. News sites, like all other content owners, still have the ability to disable the crawler via robots.txt flags. That has been the case, and it has not changed.” As of October 23, Meta has made no other changes to the terms aside from the implementation date. No versions of the terms include language about respecting robots.txt preferences. Meta's controversial terms October 15 Meta’s new commercial terms will allow the scraping of news sites, legal experts say October 17 Legal experts warn Meta’s new terms still grant broad scraping permissions, despite language changes October 21 U.K. politicians and German publishers call on regulators to pause Meta’s new terms before October 30 deadline October 22 Publishers pressure Meta to nix new terms that would let the company scrape their websites October 26 ★ Meta delays rollout of terms that would allow broad scraping of news sites. Legal experts are still skeptical. October 27 “A gross abuse of Meta’s power”: California senator Adam Schiff tells Meta to scrap its new terms of service October 28 Meta pledges to consult publishers before finalizing its new terms Legal experts and news industry leaders remain skeptical of Meta’s statements. “Publishers should always be concerned about tech companies attempting to take content for free, and should assume that Meta is taking this additional time to either find a new way to try to mislead publishers into granting Meta free access to valuable copyrighted content or finally give up in the face of pressure,” said Matt Topic , a media and intellectual property attorney at Loevy & Loevy. “Publishers don’t need more PR statements or explanations. They need Meta to keep its hands off their copyrighted material and respect publishers’ terms of service.” “I don’t think that repeating the same non-explanations for another three weeks will make much difference,” James Grimmelmann , a professor of digital and information law at Cornell Law School, added. “But maybe they’ll use the time to come up with better explanations.” Thomas Höppner , a professor and attorney specializing in competition law in Germany, told Nieman Lab that “Meta’s conduct constitutes a prima facie infringement of European Union competition law, and a textbook case for imposing interim measures.” The postponement could be a legal strategy that the company is exploring, he said. “My fear is that this is merely a measure by Meta to reduce the risk of court actions for interim measures,” Höppner said. “If affected parties wait too long from the day of knowledge of the changes [until] the filing of an action, a court will assume that the matter cannot be urgent — and deny interim relief on this ground…Under some jurisdictions, a platform needs to give business users appropriate warning before implementing measures. It may well be that Meta just tries to reduce the risk of losing on the ground not having sufficiently informed business users about its new terms.” News publishers and press associations have called on regulators to intervene before the terms take effect, and on Meta to include specific language about respecting publishers’ crawling preferences. On Thursday, the News/Media Alliance in the United States sent a letter to Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg, demanding that the company suspend the terms before October 30. The World Association of News Publishers (WAN-IFRA) sent a similar letter to Meta’s chief global affairs officer Joel Kaplan on the same day. Multiple European press associations signed a joint letter to the European Commission, citing potential violations of the Digital Markets Act and calling for intervention. Earlier this week, two German publisher associations called on the European Commission to prohibit the new terms from going into effect until a complete review has been conducted. A Commission spokesperson said in a statement to Nieman Lab on Friday the the Commission has sent “its first formal requests for information under the AI Act, including to providers that have not published summaries about the data used to train their models.” “The Commission welcomes Meta’s announcement that it has postponed the entry into force of its new commercial terms,” the statement reads. “The Commission is committed to ensuring that Meta’s practices comply in full with all applicable obligations under EU law, including Article 102 TFEU and the Digital Markets Act.” In the United Kingdom, the Competition and Market Authority sent a formal letter to Meta to discuss the terms. In a parliamentary committee session on Tuesday, executive director Will Hayter said he had a meeting scheduled with the company on Wednesday to learn more about the terms. “The CMA has been in urgent discussions with Meta this week on this important issue,” a CMA spokesperson said on Friday. “Meta has now paused implementation until 20 November. We will continue to scrutinize these terms and engage with Meta and others as we consider our next steps.” Theo Bamber, chief executive of the U.K’s News Media Association, wrote on LinkedIn on Friday that “this delay is welcome, but it is a pause not a solution.” RELATED ARTICLE Meta’s new commercial terms will allow the scraping of news sites, legal experts say Andrew Deck October 15, 2026 “What needs to happen next is for the Competition and Markets Authority, and other regulators around the world, to secure clear, public commitments from Meta to abandon the offending elements of the new Terms before they take effect,” Bamber said. Are you considering changing how you use Meta-owned platforms? Let us know: [email protected] . Get Nieman Lab&rsquo;s Daily Digest The most important stories about journalism and the future of news, delivered to your inbox every afternoon. Subscribe Hanaa' Tameez is a staff writer at Nieman Lab. You can reach her via email ( [email protected] ), Bluesky DM (@hanaatameez.bsky.social), or on Signal (@hanaatameez.01). October 26, 2026 See more on   AI & Journalism , Search & Platforms Tags AI Germany James Grimmelmann Matt Topic meta robots.txt terms of service Thomas Höppner United Kingdom The latest from Nieman Lab Press Forward gave $20 million to 205 local newsrooms over two years. Here’s how some of them spent it. The unrestricted funding helped small outlets plug holes and try new things, but publishers ran up against the limits of what two $50,000 grants could accomplish, especially when it came to hiring. Sophie Culpepper Only 32% of Americans have high confidence that news orgs will report midterm election results accurately, survey finds Sarah Scire The Nieman Lab News-About-News Quiz: October 30, 2026 Need another reason to read that 47,212th article about paywall strategy? What if it earned you a point ? Joshua Benton The New York Times is adding more AI features to its Cooking app, and users aren’t thrilled Joshua Benton The Independent Journalism Atlas looks at “wells” and “springs” in news deserts Sophie Culpepper Foundation Reports Lab Storyboard Help advance the Nieman Foundation's mission “to promote and elevate the standards of journalism” by making a donation . Subscribe Bluesky Twitter Instagram Facebook Nieman Foundation for Journalism at Harvard Walter Lippmann House, One Francis Ave. Cambridge, MA 02138 (617) 495-2237 &copy; 2026 by the President and Fellows of Harvard College Harvard University Trademark Privacy Digital Accessibility Get Nieman Lab in your inbox. All the latest headlines and insights from the world of digital journalism and publishing. Our daily newsletter is a must-read from newsrooms to executive suites. Leave this empty Email address Name (optional) Subscribe We’ll never sell your address. Prefer a weekly email? Nearly there — check your email. We’ve sent a confirmation link to . Click it and you’re on the list. Close",
