        "pageDescription": "Commission launches Creative Europe Call (EUR 3.5 M) for innovative cross-border media literacy projects, aiming to boost societal resilience against disinformation and information manipulation.",
        "evidenceText": "Call for Proposals - Cross-border Media Literacy (2027) - GFMD « Back to all Fundings Call for Proposals – Cross-border Media Literacy (2027) Organisation: European Commission Status: Open Type: Grant Theme: Media literacy Funding Size: Maximum EUR 500 000 Deadline: 20/01/2027 Eligibility: Consortia composed of at least three entities from a minimum of three different countries participating in the Creative Europe programme Target Region: Europe Application language: English Commission launches Creative Europe Call (EUR 3.5 M) for innovative cross-border media literacy projects, aiming to boost societal resilience against disinformation and information manipulation. In view of the rapidly evolving digital media landscape and the importance of equipping citizens with up-to-date skills and awareness, the European Education and Culture Executive Agency (EACEA) is publishing a call for proposals to support innovative solutions to enhance media literacy skills. The projects shall foster effective cross-border cooperation and enhance societal resilience against disinformation and information manipulation among Europeans. Support will be granted as a priority to initiatives with the potential to reach a broad audience and/or targeted groups currently underserved by media literacy programmes, with a focus on the adult population outside the education system. Eligibility The call is open to consortia composed of at least three entities from a minimum of three different countries participating in the Creative Europe programme. These consortia may include profit and/or non-profit organisations, whether private or public, such as media literacy organisations, public authorities (national, regional, local), universities and educational institutions, media organisations, information integrity organisations, research and technology institutions, technology providers, etc. Support will be granted as a priority to initiatives with the potential to reach a broad audience and/or targeted groups currently underserved by media literacy programmes, with a focus on the adult population outside the education system. Requirements The maximum co-financing rate is 70% of the total eligible costs, so applicants need to co-finance their projects. Projects should address the challenges of a continuously changing digital media landscape. Expected deliverables include creating or scaling up cross-border collaborations that support impactful and innovative approaches to media literacy based on the latest best practices. Expected deliverables include developing and deploying media literacy tools, trainings, campaigns, events and/or other initiatives targeting specific audiences, with a particular focus on adults outside education settings. Expected deliverables include assisting media literacy professionals in adapting their practices to rapidly evolving technological development, media formats and changing media consumption patterns (including the growing role of social media and influencers, AI-generated content, etc.). How to Apply Submit your proposal directly online via the Funding & Tenders Portal Electronic Submission System before 20/01/2027. Paper applications are not accepted. Apply here . For more information, please visit this page . For the latest funding opportunities, profiles of media donors, and expert insight from GFMD’s MediaDev Fundraising Guide sign up for Bottom Line newsletter Sign Up Search Search for: This website uses cookies. By continuing to use the website you consent to the use and storage of cookies on your device. You are using an outdated browser which can not show modern web content. We suggest you download Chrome or Firefox .",
